title: "Lesson and assessment — the teaching composition and the item bank over one concept page"
summary: "A lesson carries the worked example and the curricular order for one wiki concept; its assessment carries the frozen items. Neither is a second copy of the page."
---

`context/learning/<concept-id>.md` is the **lesson** for one wiki concept: the
composition that turns a page into something teachable. `context/learning/<concept-id>.assessment.md`
is the **assessment** beside it: the item bank, with the key kept out of the page
that is served to the learner. Both are documents, not a runtime — `sdt learn
lesson <concept>` composes the lesson and `sdt learn record` is the only writer of
what a learner demonstrated.

Read it with `instructions/wiki.md` (the page it teaches) and
`instructions/learning.md` (how a session runs). This module covers the artefact
only.

## What a lesson is

- **One concept, one lesson, the same id.** The lesson id equals the wiki page id
  (`context/learning/architecture/four-key-metrics.md` teaches
  `wiki/architecture/four-key-metrics`), and its `concept` field repeats it so a
  lesson read alone names its page.
- **The page owns the knowledge.** Objectives, difficulty and pacing live in the
  page's `learning:` block, and this is its whole shape:

  ```yaml
  learning:
    objectives:
      - "name the four metrics and the pipeline point each is measured at"
      - "calculate a metric over its reporting window"
    difficulty: intermediate   # introductory | intermediate | advanced
    minutes: 12                # 5-60, the review time
  ```

  A lesson never restates them: an item points at one by its 1-based index, so
  `objective: 2` means the second entry above and nothing else.
- **The lesson's own frontmatter is written by `context new --type lesson`**:
  `kind: lesson`, `uid`, `id` (equal to the concept id), `concept` (the same id,
  repeated so the file names its page), `summary`, `status`, the timestamps,
  `project`, and `links`/`sources` pointing at `wiki/<concept-id>.md`. The
  lesson's `status` is its own lifecycle, not a teaching verdict: `sdt learn
  check` judges the page.
- **A lesson is not a page.** No claim is introduced here. Every item names the
  claim it rests on (`<page-id>#claim-N`) and every example names the span it was
  re-expressed from.

## Sections, in this order and no others

`## Prerequisites` · `## Worked example` · `## Transfer`

- **Prerequisites** — the curricular order, written as what to learn first and
  why. This is not `relations.depends_on`, which is logical dependency; a lesson
  states what a learner needs before this one.
- **Worked example** — one example, carried through to its answer, with its
  provenance in `source_span` (`refs/<file>@<sha>:<start>-<end>`: the sha pins the
  converted refs file, and the numbers are **line** offsets in it). Re-express the
  example in our words and cite the span; do not paste the source. This is the one
  place where numbers, thresholds and arithmetic the page deliberately omits are
  allowed, because they are the lesson's subject. **Cite and re-express is the
  rule**: one single clause may be quoted verbatim where the exact wording carries
  the meaning (a definition, a threshold), marked as a quote with the same span;
  everything longer is re-expressed. The decision is the user's (2026-10-28).
- **Transfer** — one case the learner has not seen, posed without its solution. The
  lesson carries no item table: the items live in the assessment document below,
  so the page a learner is shown never carries the key.

## The assessment document

`context/learning/<concept-id>.assessment.md` holds the **item bank** of one
concept, beside its lesson: `kind: assessment`, `id: <concept-id>.assessment`,
`concept: <concept-id>`, then `title`, `summary`, `status` (`draft` · `active` ·
`archived`), the timestamps, `project` and `links`/`sources` at
`wiki/<concept-id>.md` — what `sdt context new --type assessment --slug <concept-id>`
writes. It may exist with no lesson — a page quizzed before it is taught:
`learn quiz`, `learn answer` and `learn record` read it either way, while
`sdt learn check` keeps a lesson among its six rules.

Its only section is `## Items`. `key_hash`, the last column, is the digest of what
decides a verdict — `expected`, `rubric`, `grading` and `distractor`, never the
prompt — so a key edit moves it and a wording edit does not. **Never compute it by
hand**: `sdt learn lesson <concept> --format json` carries each row's `key_digest`,
the value the cell holds. A cell that disagrees with its row is refused on read
(quiz, answer, record) and reported by `sdt context lint`.

## The item table

```markdown
| id | objective | type | prompt | expected | rubric | distractor | claim | grading | provenance | frozen | hash | key_hash |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| four-key-metrics-1 | 1 | recall | Name the four metrics. | deployment frequency; lead time for changes; change failure rate; time to restore service | all four, no extras | the four are individual performance targets | architecture/four-key-metrics#c-ab12cd34 | exact | material | 2026-10-28 | 4af036062f63 | 9f1c2d7a4b06 |
```

- **`objective`** is 1-based against the page's `learning.objectives`; every
  objective needs at least one item, and an index outside the list is a defect.
- **`id`** is free but unique and stable: an attempt cites it, so renaming one
  orphans the answer that used it.
- **`prompt`** asks the question and carries no part of the answer; **`rubric`**
  says what a complete answer shows; **`grading`** is `auto`, `exact`, `numeric`,
  `keywords` or `manual` (`auto` picks from the item's shape); **`provenance`** is
  `material`, `ai_generated`, `mixed` or `unknown`; **`frozen`** is the day the key
  was written.
- **`claim`** is `<page-id>#c-<id>`, where `<page-id>` is the id **without** the
  `wiki/` prefix and `c-<id>` is the claim's stable id
  (`architecture/four-key-metrics#c-ab12cd34`). An item whose claim does not
  support its expected answer is the defect this column exists to surface. The
  positional `#claim-N` is the legacy form, reported until migrated.
- **`hash`** pins the claim's digest, the fingerprint of the text the item was
  written against. Read it from `sdt learn lesson <concept> --format json` (the
  `claims` array) or from the `claim <id> <digest>` line of the text form.
  `sdt learn lesson --validate` reports `stale` when the claim now says something
  else, `unverified` when no digest was pinned — a finding to clear before
  shipping — and `broken` when the id is gone.
- **`type`** is `recall`, `diagnose`, `apply` or `transfer`, and it *is* the
  item's difficulty ladder in that order, never a number. The page's `difficulty`
  paces the lesson; the two are not the same scale. An `apply` item may rest on the
  worked example, and its rubric says so.
- **`expected`** is the answer, not a hint: precise for recall, with its tolerance
  for a numeric apply (`2/36 = 5.56% (±0.1 percentage points)`). State unit and
  rounding once; a rate over one day and the reported metric over its window are
  two numbers, so an item says which one it asks for. An answer the page does not
  determine is marked **not in the corpus**, never filled from recall. **Write it
  from the page, never from the span**: the worked example may carry what the page
  leaves out, an item's key may not.
- **`distractor`** is a real wrong model — the page's counter-case or a
  boundary — never a random option, and never the expected answer.
- At least one item per concept is `transfer`: the same idea in a case the page did
  not cover. The `## Transfer` **section** poses open work for a session to run; the
  `transfer` **item** is its assessable form — one section, one such item.

## Check before you ship

```bash
sdt learn lesson <concept>              # the composed bundle
sdt learn lesson --validate             # every lesson: claim anchors, objective coverage, shape
sdt learn lesson --coverage             # objective → item matrix
sdt learn check <concept>               # is this concept teachable, and if not why
```

`sdt learn check` refuses a page that cannot carry a lesson — not `active`, fewer
than four claims, `maturity: wip`, no `learning:` block, no lesson, an objective
without an item. It judges the **page**; a lesson's own `status` is its lifecycle
and is not part of the verdict. A **missing `verified_at` is not a refusal**: a
stamp is provenance, not validity, and it is reported as a note. `--verified-only`
filters on it when a caller wants a freshness subset.

## Boundaries

- One lesson per concept; a second lesson for the same page is a split signal, not
  a variant.
- No answers stored in the page, no objectives stored here, no learner state in
  either: `context/learning/learner.yaml` holds what a learner demonstrated.
- Never promote a page to `active` because a lesson needs it. The page is verified
  against its spans first (`wiki.md`), and the lesson follows.
